      </a>
      <span class="blog-post__tag">Document</span>
      <h1 class="blog-post__title">I send a PDF to my AI. It signs and returns it.</h1>
      <p class="blog-post__subtitle">During Asia/US contract season, I need to sign multiple contracts every day. My AI handles it — using my saved signature, it signs PDFs and sends them back in seconds.</p>
      <div class="blog-post__meta">
        <span>April 2026</span>
        <span>·</span>
        <span>2 min read</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="blog-post__hero-image">
      <img src="/images/blog-contract-signing.png" alt="AI contract signing workflow">
    </div>

    <div class="blog-post__content">
      <p>It's contract season. Between Asia and US time zones, contracts come in at all hours — supplier agreements, NDAs, service contracts. On a busy day, I might need to sign 10-15 documents.</p>

      <p>Here's what I used to do: download the PDF, open Preview, add my signature, save, rename, attach to reply email, send. Maybe 3-4 minutes per contract. On a heavy day, that's close to an hour just signing things.</p>

      <h2>What I do now</h2>

      <p>I forward the contract PDF to my AI assistant with a simple message:</p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>"Sign this contract and send it back."</p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>That's it. My AI:</p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Receives the PDF</strong> — I forward it via email or WhatsApp</li>
        <li><strong>Opens and reads it</strong> — confirms it's a contract, not spam</li>
        <li><strong>Applies my saved signature</strong> — stored securely, placed in the right position</li>
        <li><strong>Returns the signed PDF</strong> — ready to send, often with the reply already drafted</li>
      </ol>

      <p>From start to finish: about 15 seconds. No downloading, no printing, no scanning.</p>

      <h2>The setup</h2>

      <p>This isn't magic — it's a configured workflow. During setup, I:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Saved my digital signature once (PNG with transparent background)</li>
        <li>Told my AI where signatures typically go on contracts I receive</li>
        <li>Set it to always flag contracts with unusual terms before signing</li>
      </ul>

      <p>After that, it just works. Every time.</p>

      <div class="highlight-box">
        <p><strong>Time saved per month:</strong> ~5 hours</p>
        <p><strong>Contracts signed this season:</strong> 40+</p>
        <p><strong>Mistakes:</strong> 0</p>
      </div>

      <h2>Who is this for?</h2>

      <p>Anyone who signs documents regularly:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Procurement managers</strong> — supplier contracts, purchase orders</li>
        <li><strong>Business owners</strong> — NDAs, service agreements, lease renewals</li>
        <li><strong>Real estate agents</strong> — tenancy agreements, sale &amp; purchase forms</li>
        <li><strong>Consultants</strong> — engagement letters, scope of work documents</li>
      </ul>

      <p>If you're signing more than 5 contracts a week, this workflow alone pays for itself.</p>

      <h2>Not just signing</h2>

      <p>The same AI also:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Summarizes contract terms before I sign</li>
        <li>Flags unusual clauses or expiry dates</li>
        <li>Organizes signed copies into folders automatically</li>
        <li>Sends reminders before contracts expire</li>
      </ul>

      <p>It's not just faster — it's smarter than doing it manually.</p>
    </div>

